For every triangle $T=\{e,f,g\}\in\mathcal T$ and $t\in[0,1]$, the independent unfolding satisfies $$\label{eq:pair}
 \mathbb P(\text{the pair $\{f,g\}\subset T$ returns true at $t$})
   =q_{f,T}(t)q_{g,T}(t),$$ $$\label{eq:products}
 \begin{aligned}
 q_e(t)&=\prod_{S\ni e}\bigl(1-I_{e,S}(t)\bigr),\\
 q_{e,T}(t)&=\prod_{\substack{S\ni e\\S\ne T}}
                    \bigl(1-I_{e,S}(t)\bigr).
 \end{aligned}$$ In the pair probability, $T$ is omitted from the pair’s candidate list. All products are over $\mathcal T$, and an empty product equals $1$. The functions $q_e$, $q_{e,T}$, and $I_{e,T}$ belong to $C^1([0,1])$. Moreover, $q_e(0)=q_{e,T}(0)=1$ and $I_{e,T}(0)=0$.

There are fixed constants $C_1,J<\infty$ such that every graph $G$ satisfying the degree, codegree and link-cycle conditions of Proposition <a href="#prop:prefix" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:prefix">[prop:prefix]</a> obeys $$\label{eq:semigroup}
 \sup_{0\le s\le\log n}
 \left\lVert e^{-sA/D}\right\rVert_\infty
 \le C_1(1+\log n)^J$$ for all sufficiently large $n$.

hypotheses.statement:
For every integer $h\geq 1$, every ordered copy of $H$ in $A_h$ maps the pattern entry $(s+1,s+1)$ into $$\mathcal L_h
 =\bigcup_{p=0}^{m-1} R_h^+(p)\times C_h^+(p).$$ In particular, $|\mathcal L_h|=m$ and $$\label{eq:copy-bound}
 N_H(A_h)\leq m n^{2k-2},
 \qquad
 \frac{N_H(A_h)}{n^{2k}}
 \leq \frac{2^{-h}}{d_h^2}.$$

